// Fixture: flags_width_overflow_error
// Rule under test: flags/bN design -- "a flags domain whose real bit-flag members
// exceed what its declared width can address is a compile-time error", same SHAPE
// as identifier's indexed_width_overflow check but a genuinely different formula:
// identifier's dense index can address 2**bits distinct values, but a bitmask can
// only address `bits` real positions. 'u8' has 8 addressable bit positions; nine
// auto-assigned real members (the 'none' sentinel doesn't count -- it claims no bit)
// need a ninth position that doesn't exist.

flags X u8
	none = 0
	a
	b
	c
	d
	e
	f
	g
	h
	i

// Expected: real, hard compile-time error (phase 4, check: flags_width_overflow).
//   Domain 'X' declares width 'u8' (8 bits), but member 'i' (the 9th real member,
//   a..h already having claimed bits 0..7) has no unclaimed bit left to
//   auto-assign. Domain-only fixture, no instances needed, `instances` is empty.
